  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "The chef tastes the spiked tea and immediately recognizes the hidden alcohol. He praises his sous-chef for the clever trick while Kostya delivers a note to Victoria.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Отличный чай!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А что это за чай-то?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Это Сенча.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Молодцы, действуем по нашему плану.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Давай, Костян, давай, давай!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "angelina",
      "t": "Всё понятно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Займись седьмым столиком, пожалуйста.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "angelina",
      "t": "Виктория Сергеевна, это записка вам.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Этот чай очень хорошо успокаивает.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Я рад, что вам понравилось.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Заварите мне ещё одну чашку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Сейчас всё сделаем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "angelina",
      "t": "А от кого эта записка?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Просто передай её Виктории Сергеевне.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Отличный чай!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Excellent tea!' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'excellent' straight to 'tea'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А что это за чай-то?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what kind of tea is this?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'tea'. Then Lyova responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Это Сенча.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is Sencha.' Lyova links 'this' directly with 'sencha' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Молодцы, действуем по нашему плану.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Good job, we are acting according to our plan.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'well done' with 'plan'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Давай, Костян, давай, давай!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come on, Kostyan, come on, come on!' The key word here means 'let's', which Lyova combines with 'Kostyan'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "angelina",
      "t": "Всё понятно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Everything is understood.' Kostya combines 'everything' with 'clear' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Lyova responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Займись седьмым столиком, пожалуйста.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Take care of the seventh table, please.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'to attend to' with 'please'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "angelina",
      "t": "Виктория Сергеевна, это записка вам.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Victoria Sergeyevna, this is a note for you.' Kostya uses the polite plural 'you' alongside 'you', keeping a formal or respectful register.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Этот чай очень хорошо успокаивает.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This tea calms very well.' Notice how 'this' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'calm down'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Я рад, что вам понравилось.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am glad that you liked it.' Lyova combines 'I' with 'pleased' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Заварите мне ещё одну чашку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Brew me one more cup.' Chef Viktor links 'brew' directly with 'to me' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Сейчас всё сделаем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We will do everything now.' In Russian, 'now' leads into 'we'll do' in a very natural everyday phrasing. Then Kostya replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "angelina",
      "t": "А от кого эта записка?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And from whom is this note?' Kostya links 'and' directly with 'whom' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Просто передай её Виктории Сергеевне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Just pass it to Victoria Sergeyevna.' Notice how 'just' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'Sergeyevna'. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
